# Archives dropped into the working directory to be unpacked.
# The unpacked result belongs in the tree; the archive does not, and one
# already reached a branch through a `git add -A` that meant well.
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.tgz

# Pages fetched from vendor and distributor sites while researching a part.
# Same failure as the archives above and it happened a second time: a scraped
# Adafruit page reached `main` in #98 through `git add -A` from the repository
# root. What belongs in the tree is the *reading* of a source in
# docs/research/, identified by URL and revision — never the source itself,
# which is somebody else's copyrighted page. Only the two site files at
# docs/404.html and docs/index.html are tracked HTML; anything at the root is
# a stray.
/*.html
/*.htm
/*.pdf

# Private notes — not for publication
EASTER_EGGS.md

# CTest scratch. `ctest` writes Testing/Temporary/ next to the source tree
# whenever it is run from the repository root; none of it describes the project.
#
# **Anchored -- and see the note under `/artifacts/` below, which is the same
# argument and applies to that line too.** Without the leading slash gitignore(5)
# matches the name at any depth, so a vendored `components/*/Testing/` or a
# future `docs/hardware/artifacts/` would vanish from `git add -A` -- this hunk's
# own failure mode, inverted. `Testing/` unanchored is worse than that on a
# case-insensitive checkout, where it also swallows `docs/testing/`.
#
# **The hazard is a file that arrives there, not the ones already in it.**
# gitignore reaches untracked paths only, so `docs/testing/HIL_PLANS.md`, which
# is tracked on `main`, stays tracked under either spelling and its edits still
# stage. What an unanchored `Testing/` costs is the **next** file in that
# directory -- `docs/testing/WATCH_CONTROL.md` arriving with #121, the file
# CLAUDE.md sends every agent to -- which would never be offered by `git add -A`
# and would go unnoticed, `docs/` being the one prefix the unattended merge sweep
# may touch.
#
# Checked rather than asserted, because the mechanism has two traps in it. A
# scratch repository, `git check-ignore -v --no-index` (without `--no-index` it
# consults the index and calls any *tracked* path not-ignored, whichever patterns
# are in force, so it answers the same for both spellings and proves nothing),
# and `-c core.ignorecase=true` (this working tree has it true, being NTFS under
# WSL, but a case-sensitive checkout does not and would not exercise the fold at
# all). Under those two: unanchored `Testing/` matches `docs/testing/HIL_PLANS.md`
# and `docs/testing/WATCH_CONTROL.md`; anchored `/Testing/` matches neither.
#
# **#121 adds these same two names unanchored, in this same place in this same
# file, from this same base.** Whichever merges second conflicts, and gitignore
# is additive -- keeping both hunks leaves the unanchored pair in force and
# recreates exactly what this comment argues against. **These two lines replace
# them; they do not join them.**
#
# Written here rather than in a pull request body because a squash merge keeps
# the file and discards the body. **And a comment is all it is: nothing enforces
# it.** Resolve the conflict wrongly and the failure is silent, and silent only
# where it matters -- an unanchored `Testing/` swallows `docs/testing/` on a
# case-insensitive checkout, which the owner has and CI does not, so CI would
# never show it. Filed as **T-159**: a check that fails when this file carries an
# unanchored `Testing` or `artifacts` entry, so the rule is a red build rather
# than a paragraph somebody has to read while resolving a conflict. Raised in
# review of https://github.com/hleserg/Attadipa/pull/134 and not built there,
# because the only home CI already runs it from needs a line in
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, which an agent token cannot write.
/Testing/

# Screenshots and scenario output from the UI test tooling: evidence for a
# session, not source. They are regenerated by re-running whatever wrote them,
# and a megabyte of PNG per commit is what this line is for. (The tool itself
# arrives with #121. The *first* line below stands without it -- a directory of
# screenshots is worth ignoring whoever writes it. The second does not: it is
# derived from `tools/watch_control.py`'s CWD-relative default, which exists
# only on that branch, and an earlier version of this comment claimed the whole
# entry was independent of #121. It is not, and saying so is cheaper than a
# reader discovering it while checking the claim.)
#
# **Anchored, for the reason spelled out above `/Testing/`** -- repeated here in
# one line because whoever next edits this entry will be looking at this entry,
# not at that one, and an argument that lives only next to its sibling is an
# argument that gets dropped. Unanchored, `artifacts/` would also hide a future
# `docs/hardware/artifacts/`. #121 adds this name unanchored; this replaces it.
#
# **And the anchor assumes the tool is run from the repository root**, the same
# assumption `/Testing/` states for `ctest` above. It is not always true:
# `tools/watch_control.py` defaults its output to `artifacts/watch` relative to
# the *process* working directory, so `cd sim && python3 ../tools/watch_control.py
# screenshot` writes `sim/artifacts/watch/*.png`, which `/artifacts/` does not
# match and the next `git add -A` at the root would carry -- a fourth prefix for
# the recurrence T-117 is written about, one directory from the one just closed.
# So the second line, which catches that directory at any depth. On its own it
# would also hide `docs/hardware/artifacts/watch/`, by construction -- it
# matches the name at every depth and cannot tell whose it is -- and that is a
# real cost rather than a theoretical one: `docs/hardware/` is where bench
# evidence goes AND a prefix the unattended sweep may merge, so an agent writing
# a screenshot there would lose it inside a sweep-merged commit with nothing
# red. The third line buys it back.
#
# BOTH DIRECTIONS COST SOMETHING, and the third line is the one that has to say
# so. Losing a screenshot costs an agent a re-run. Un-ignoring a directory under
# `docs/` costs the other way round, and it costs more: `docs/hardware/` is on
# ATTADIPA_MERGE_ALLOWED_PREFIXES, `docs/` is the published Pages root, and
# T-117 item 1 records that nothing in `.github/workflows/` looks at what a pull
# request adds in BYTES. So a `git add -A` that sweeps up a stray PNG puts
# megabytes on `main` and on the public site with nothing red -- which is this
# branch's own 3.3 MB accident with the unattended sweep added. The carve-out is
# therefore as narrow as it can be made: ONE anchored directory, not a `**` glob.
#
# `!docs/**/artifacts/watch/` was the first spelling and it was too wide.
# gitignore(5): a `/**/` matches zero or more directories, so it also un-ignores
# `docs/artifacts/watch/` -- and `cd docs && python3 ../tools/watch_control.py
# screenshot` lands exactly there. That is the same CWD assumption the paragraph
# above faults `/artifacts/` for making about `sim/`, one directory over. Found
# by review of https://github.com/hleserg/Attadipa/pull/134, round fourteen.
#
# Verified in a scratch repository with `check-ignore`, both spellings, before
# and after: `artifacts/watch/a.png` ignored, `sim/artifacts/watch/b.png`
# ignored, `docs/hardware/artifacts/watch/c.png` VISIBLE under both --
# and `docs/artifacts/watch/e.png` VISIBLE under `docs/**/`, ignored under the
# anchored spelling, which is the hole and its close.
/artifacts/
**/artifacts/watch/
!docs/hardware/artifacts/watch/
